## Search (Prospect)

- `bootstrap/app.rs` installs Prospect with `.prospect(app::providers::search::register)`; `app/providers/search.rs`
  registers the searchable models above `// smeltery:searchables`. `PROSPECT_DRIVER=database` (in `.env`) uses the
  database's own full-text index (SQLite FTS5, PostgreSQL `tsvector`, MySQL `FULLTEXT`), kept current by the
  database itself, raw SQL included.
- `smeltery make:model Post title:string body:text --searchable` (with `-m` / `--all`: the index goes into the
  create-table migration; without: a separate `add_search_index_to_posts_table` migration) writes `impl Searchable`
  (every `string` / `text` field searched, the first with `Weight::A`; `foreign` fields as filters) and registers
  the model; with `-r` / `--all` the list page searches (`?q=`, `?page=`, the label highlighted) behind
  `throttle:60,1`.
- In a handler: `Post::search(&prospect, &q)` (`prospect: smeltery::prospect::Prospect`,
  `use smeltery::prospect::Searchable as _`), then `.within(…)` for a `scoped_by` model, `.where_eq(…)` on declared
  filters, `.highlight([...])`, `.paginate(page)` → `Page<Hit<Post>>`. User text is never search syntax.
- A search finds every record the model's spec allows: scope private records (`i.scoped_by`, `i.only_when`,
  `.where_eq`). Highlights are text segments (`{"text", "matched"}`): render them escaped, wrap matches in `<mark>`.
- `impl Searchable` and the migration's `SearchIndex` must name the same columns and weights; change both with a
  migration that calls `SearchIndex::on(…).rebuild(schema)`. `smeltery prospect:status` shows each index.

{% endif -%}
{% if hallmark -%}
## API tokens (Hallmark)

- `bootstrap/app.rs` installs Hallmark with `.hallmark(Hallmark::new())` (after Temper's user model): the guard
  `hallmark`, the middleware `abilities:` / `ability:`, the `Tokens` service and the console command
  `hallmark:prune-expired`. The table is `personal_access_tokens` (migration `create_personal_access_tokens_table`).
- `routes/api.rs`: `POST /api/tokens` (`throttle:10,1`; `email`, `password`, `device_name`, and `code` when the
  account has two-factor authentication on) issues a token through `smeltery::hallmark::issue_for_credentials` and
  answers 201 `{"token":"smt_…","token_type":"Bearer",…}`; `DELETE /api/tokens/current` signs the presenting token out;
  `GET /api/user` (with `verified`) shows its user. The controllers are in `app/controllers/api/`. With
  `.verify_email` on, `verified` answers 403 to tokens of unverified addresses: add it to API routes that need a
  verified address.
- API routes use `.middleware("auth:hallmark")`, then `abilities:a,b` (every listed ability) or `ability:a,b` (at
  least one). Handlers take `who: smeltery::auth::Authenticated` (`who.user_id`, `who.can("…")`) or
  `token: smeltery::hallmark::CurrentToken`. Abilities limit tokens, not users: handlers still check the record.
- Tokens travel only in `Authorization: Bearer smt_…` (never a query string, cookie or form field); only their hash
  is stored; never log, flash or store a plain token. A password change ends the user's tokens.
- `HALLMARK_TOKEN_EXPIRATION` (days, 365), `HALLMARK_SPA` (a JavaScript frontend on this app's origin calls the API
  with its session) and `HALLMARK_STATEFUL` are commented in `.env`.{% if agents %} `app/agents/mod.rs` schedules
  `hallmark-prune` daily (expired tokens leave the table).{% endif %}
- Tests: `smeltery::hallmark::testing::acting_as(&app, &user, &["…"])` sends a real token with every following
  request; `tests/api_tokens.rs` creates its users with `UserFactory`.

{% endif -%}
{% if anvil -%}
## Broadcasting (Anvil)

- `bootstrap/app.rs` installs Anvil with `.anvil(routes::channels::channels)`: a WebSocket endpoint on the app's own
  port (`/app/<ANVIL_APP_KEY>`, the Pusher protocol 7){% if auth %}, `POST /broadcasting/auth` (session and CSRF) for private
  channels{% endif %}, `POST /api/broadcasting/auth` for clients with a bearer token{% if not hallmark %} (it answers 404 until the
  app has a bearer guard such as Hallmark's API tokens){% endif %}, and the `Anvil` service.
- `routes/channels.rs` declares the channels: `c.public("announcements")` (anyone with the app key may subscribe, so
  only public data goes there){% if auth %} and `c.private("users.{user}", …)`, which lets only that user join
  `private-users.<id>`; every private channel checks that the signed-in user may see the record{% endif %}. Add channels above
  `// smeltery:channels`.
- Events are structs in `app/events/` with `#[derive(Serialize, BroadcastEvent)]` and
  `#[broadcast(public = "…")]`{% if auth %} / `#[broadcast(private = "users.{user_id}")]`{% endif %}; `app/events/announcement_posted.rs`
  is the example. Send with `anvil.send(&event).await?` (a handler takes `anvil: smeltery::anvil::Anvil`; jobs and
  agents use `smeltery::anvil::Anvil::of(&app)`); clients receive the JSON under `App\Events\<TypeName>`. Send after a
  transaction commits.
- The key and the signing secret are derived from `APP_KEY` (the `ANVIL_*` lines in `.env` are commented). Set
  `ANVIL_APP_KEY` when clients are built with it, because a new `APP_KEY` changes the derived key.
  `PUBSUB_DRIVER` carries events between processes (`serve --no-agents` and `work`); the `pubsub_messages`
  migration is there for its `database` driver.
{%- if alloy %}
- The {{ kit_name }} pages use Echo (`{% if react %}@laravel/echo-react{% else %}@laravel/echo-vue{% endif %}`, `laravel-echo`, `pusher-js`, pinned in
  `package.json`). `resources/js/echo.ts` configures it once (`startEcho`, called from `{{ entry }}` with the shared prop
  `app.anvil_key`): the socket goes to the page's own host and port, private and presence channels are authorized at
  `/broadcasting/auth` with the session cookie and the `XSRF-TOKEN` cookie as `X-XSRF-TOKEN`, and Inertia's requests
  carry `X-Socket-ID` (for `.except(socket)`). In a component: `useEchoPublic("announcements", "AnnouncementPosted",
  cb)`, `useEcho("users." + id, "UserNotified", cb)` (private), `echo().join("dashboard").here(…).joining(…).leaving(…)`
  (presence). Examples: `resources/js/components/{% if react %}announcements.tsx{% else %}AnnouncementsCard.vue{% endif %}` on the welcome page{% if auth %}, and
  `{% if react %}live-notifications.tsx{% else %}LiveNotifications.vue{% endif %}` on the dashboard (the private channel, "Notify me" → `POST /notify-me`, and how many
  people are online through `presence-dashboard`, plus the viewer's own name; the channel shares member ids only, and
  `routes/channels.rs` shows the one line that shares names; its members' tables come from the `create_presence_tables`
  migration){% endif %}.
{%- else %}
- Mold pages listen without JavaScript: a `#[spark(stream)]` component's method marked
  `#[on("anvil:<channel>", "App\\Events\\<Event>")]` runs when that event is broadcast (`smeltery make:spark Name
  --listen <channel> --event <Event>` writes one). Examples: `app/sparks/announcements.rs` on the home page{% if auth %}, and
  `app/sparks/notifications.rs` on the dashboard (the private `private-users.{user_id}`; its "Notify me" action sends
  the event){% endif %}. Other clients (mobile apps, scripts) use `pusher-js` or a Pusher client library with the key.
{%- endif %}
- Tests: `smeltery::anvil::testing` (`AnvilSpy`, `TestSocket`{% if auth %}, `authorize`{% endif %}); see `tests/broadcasting.rs`.

